1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They can be as small as a pea or as large as a bowling ball.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Nutritional, durable, and versatile.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The ancients worshipped them.

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Imperial navies were sustained by them.

5
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
And tomorrow, they just might feed the world.

6
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
From peanut butter to biofuel.

7
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
From tree shaking to high-speed cracking.

8
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
From candied treats to cleaning the space shuttle.

9
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
It's all nuts on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Diamond Foods, Stockton, California.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,060
Here in this 550,000 square foot facility, more walnuts are processed daily than any

12
00:00:56,061 --> 00:00:59,060
other place in the world.

13
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:09,460
We receive roughly 260 million pounds of walnuts each year, and we shell about 220 million pounds, making roughly 80, 90 million pounds of kernels.

14
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:16,616
Located in the heart of California's Central Valley, where 98% of all U.S.

15
00:01:16,617 --> 00:01:21,180
walnuts are grown, Diamond has been in the nut business for more than 90 years.

16
00:01:21,181 --> 00:01:29,040
Begun as a farmer's co-op in 1912, Diamond has been a leader in the transformation of

17
00:01:29,041 --> 00:01:32,640
this once cottage industry into an agribusiness powerhouse.

18
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:40,460
Americans consume more than 1 billion pounds of nuts annually, and the numbers are on the

19
00:01:40,461 --> 00:01:41,161
rise.

20
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,400
The world loves nuts.

21
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:48,860
Nuts are nutrition-packed with a lot of minerals and vitamins.

22
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:54,700
For example, walnuts contains the highest amount of omega-3 in any plant source.

23
00:01:55,080 --> 00:02:00,160
And studies have shown that eating nuts will actually reduce your cholesterol

24
00:02:00,161 --> 00:02:02,800
and avoid some cardiovascular problems.

25
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,760
But as commonplace as nuts have become in our daily regimen,

26
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:10,800
the truth is most of us can't answer the most basic questions about them.

27
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,560
In fact, what exactly is a nut?

28
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,580
Take almonds and pistachios.

29
00:02:16,581 --> 00:02:22,180
Technically, they're a droop, not a nut, and grow more like a peach.

30
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:24,960
Peanuts? A legume.

31
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:28,160
And botanically speaking, more pea than nut.

32
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:32,300
Brazil nuts? Pine nuts? Cashews?

33
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:36,300
Sorry, technically none of them meet the botanical definition.

34
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:42,300
The botany is important because of the structures which give rise to your kernel,

35
00:02:42,301 --> 00:02:46,240
which give rise to that seed coat around the kernel, which give rise to the shell and such.

36
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:49,880
from a culinary sense or from a day-to-day sense, it's a seed that's edible.

37
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:55,980
That means if you plant a nut, a tree or a plant will grow, if properly tended.

38
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,800
You could probably even take a walnut from the supermarket

39
00:02:59,801 --> 00:03:05,540
and bury it in a couple inches of soil and start a little walnut tree.

40
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,040
Keeping it going would be something else, and if they'd been overheated,

41
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:11,560
they'd be hard to grow, but it has been done.

42
00:03:11,561 --> 00:03:17,560
In the United States, the rise of the American nut industry is a relatively recent phenomenon,

43
00:03:17,561 --> 00:03:21,560
given that nuts are steeped in the ancient histories of Europe, Asia and the Middle East.

44
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:31,560
Organized piles of petrified walnut shells have been found in Iran that date back to 7,000 B.C.

45
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:37,560
And ancient Romans so loved walnuts that the Latin name means belonging to royalty.

46
00:03:37,561 --> 00:03:38,422
royalty.

47
00:03:38,423 --> 00:03:47,580
They were actually known as the food for the gods and were associated with fertility.

48
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:54,480
Like the walnut, the almond is an ancient émigré carried from its native Asia by travelers along

49
00:03:54,481 --> 00:03:55,688
the fabled Silk Roads.

50
00:03:55,689 --> 00:03:59,420
One of the ways you can mark the Silk Road is look at where you have

51
00:03:59,421 --> 00:04:00,647
almond trees being produced.

52
00:04:00,648 --> 00:04:03,320
So if you go back now through the Atlas Mountains of Morocco,

53
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:07,400
for instance, you'll find all of these almonds which were brought in initially from the Silk

54
00:04:07,401 --> 00:04:09,640
world travelers.

55
00:04:09,641 --> 00:04:12,140
Phoenician and Arab traders eventually spread almonds

56
00:04:12,141 --> 00:04:14,080
throughout Europe.

57
00:04:14,081 --> 00:04:16,640
Because of their shell, they are well protected.

58
00:04:16,641 --> 00:04:19,560
So if you keep them in a reasonable place,

59
00:04:19,561 --> 00:04:23,580
you can keep nuts in good shape, ready to eat,

60
00:04:23,581 --> 00:04:25,180
for quite a length of time.

61
00:04:25,181 --> 00:04:28,580
Just about every nut has traveled to a different location

62
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:31,560
in a different country.

63
00:04:31,561 --> 00:04:34,080
Both walnuts and almonds were first brought to America

64
00:04:34,081 --> 00:04:37,160
by Spanish missionaries traveling California's Camino

65
00:04:37,161 --> 00:04:42,160
in the 18th century.

66
00:04:42,161 --> 00:04:44,160
But until well into the 20th century,

67
00:04:44,161 --> 00:04:48,160
almond and walnut orchards struggled in the coastal climates,

68
00:04:48,161 --> 00:04:50,160
where the missions had been built.

69
00:04:50,161 --> 00:04:54,160
After World War II, as Southern California commercial

70
00:04:54,161 --> 00:04:58,160
and real estate development sort of took over some of the farmland,

71
00:04:58,161 --> 00:05:02,160
farmers moved to the fertile soils of the San Joaquin

72
00:05:02,161 --> 00:05:07,160
and Sacramento Valleys, and that's where our nuts are grown today.

73
00:05:07,161 --> 00:05:15,820
Today, walnuts and almonds thrive in the Central Valley's Mediterranean climate, with its

74
00:05:15,821 --> 00:05:19,400
sun-drenched growing season.

75
00:05:19,401 --> 00:05:24,460
Each spring, millions of trees within these vast orchards begin to pollinate, the first

76
00:05:24,461 --> 00:05:27,000
stage in the development of the nuts.

77
00:05:27,001 --> 00:05:34,480
The female flower is in here, nestled in the leaves, and what you would see is the two

78
00:05:34,481 --> 00:05:40,240
stigma surfaces which are moist and are out there ready to catch the pollen that's flying

79
00:05:40,241 --> 00:05:41,598
through the air.

80
00:05:41,599 --> 00:05:47,880
The pollen comes from these flowers, this is a catkin, and mass quantities

81
00:05:47,881 --> 00:05:52,640
of pollen can be shed from just a few catkins.

82
00:05:52,641 --> 00:05:57,660
While walnuts are pollinated by winds that scatter pollen for hundreds of miles, almonds

83
00:05:57,661 --> 00:05:58,887
are bee pollinated.

84
00:05:58,888 --> 00:06:03,600
And because almond trees are fertile for only 10 to 20 days every spring,

85
00:06:03,601 --> 00:06:06,200
must reach out to beekeepers around the world.

86
00:06:06,201 --> 00:06:10,600
A real challenge, this is February here in the Central Valley, so we can have lots of

87
00:06:10,601 --> 00:06:12,690
rainstorms, lots of winds, frosts and such.

88
00:06:12,691 --> 00:06:14,780
The number of days those bees actually move

89
00:06:14,781 --> 00:06:17,370
can be just a small proportion of those 20 days.

90
00:06:17,371 --> 00:06:19,420
So it makes it a real interesting time

91
00:06:19,421 --> 00:06:22,280
for the growers.

92
00:06:22,281 --> 00:06:26,840
Every year, one million bee colonies are needed to pollinate California's almonds.

93
00:06:26,841 --> 00:06:31,980
We're having beekeepers from far away as Florida and the Midwest, or even some from

94
00:06:31,981 --> 00:06:35,920
Australia this last year to fill the supply.

95
00:06:35,921 --> 00:06:42,660
Once the females are pollinated, the flowers fall away and small green hubs begin to grow.

96
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:47,380
By April, the almond fruit will develop to full size, leaving the more significant changes

97
00:06:47,381 --> 00:06:50,940
to occur within, in a process known as specialization.

98
00:06:50,941 --> 00:06:56,840
When you look at an almond, developing almond, you cut through, and at this stage, the nut

99
00:06:56,841 --> 00:06:57,840
hasn't hardened yet.

100
00:06:57,841 --> 00:07:00,880
You see this white part, it's starting to become lignified, it's starting to become

101
00:07:00,881 --> 00:07:03,575
hard and this will become the hole in the almond.

102
00:07:03,576 --> 00:07:05,720
And this white part is already starting

103
00:07:05,721 --> 00:07:07,978
to become the stone or the shell of the almond.

104
00:07:07,979 --> 00:07:10,380
But also in the center part you see the developing

105
00:07:10,381 --> 00:07:15,420
seed and you can see the seed coat here on the outside and then the kernel developing

106
00:07:15,421 --> 00:07:17,420
on the inside.

107
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:21,720
By the fall the kernel and the shell will be fully formed, protected by a leathery

108
00:07:21,721 --> 00:07:29,091
hull. Harvest begins in August for almonds and in September for walnuts.

109
00:07:29,092 --> 00:07:30,320
All the nuts

110
00:07:30,321 --> 00:07:34,720
processed and sold throughout the following year are gathered during these intense eight-week

111
00:07:34,721 --> 00:07:35,421
periods.

112
00:07:35,422 --> 00:07:41,884
It used to be the varieties were bigger, there weren't as many nuts per tree.

113
00:07:41,885 --> 00:07:42,980
Now they come

114
00:07:42,981 --> 00:07:48,340
into bearing quicker, they have better color, lighter color, they're more disease resistant.

115
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:52,101
That's where we've embraced technology and science.

116
00:07:52,102 --> 00:07:54,800
Historically, walnut harvests were labor

117
00:07:54,801 --> 00:07:59,600
intensive, requiring large crews who literally shook the nuts from the trees by hand.

118
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:07,200
You had people climb the tree, and they had what's called a mallet, which is basically

119
00:08:07,201 --> 00:08:09,405
a stick with a big rubber head on it.

120
00:08:09,406 --> 00:08:12,980
They would literally beat the limbs until the nuts fell out.

121
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:19,520
The second way would they have long poles, 10, 15, 20 feet poles, and they would tap the nuts.

122
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:34,260
Today, the nut industry utilizes mechanical shakers that feature powerful hydraulic arms,

123
00:08:34,261 --> 00:08:37,040
which extend around the trunk to rattle the nuts free.

124
00:08:37,041 --> 00:08:39,020
It's the coolest thing in the world.

125
00:08:39,021 --> 00:08:40,840
It's like an earthquake.

126
00:08:40,841 --> 00:08:42,460
You can feel the earth move.

127
00:08:42,461 --> 00:08:44,480
I mean, everybody should feel it.

128
00:08:44,481 --> 00:08:50,240
You grab the tree and you, can I say, you shake the hell out of it?

129
00:08:50,241 --> 00:08:54,180
Once the nuts have fallen to the ground, a windrow machine funnels them into narrow

130
00:08:54,181 --> 00:08:56,080
rows to be picked up by a sweeper.

131
00:08:59,820 --> 00:09:02,040
At this point, most nuts are still in their protective

132
00:09:02,041 --> 00:09:03,420
hulls, which must be removed.

133
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:09,420
Historically, these hulls have served many uses,

134
00:09:09,421 --> 00:09:12,800
including as a source of dye for uniforms worn by Confederate

135
00:09:12,801 --> 00:09:15,720
soldiers in the American Civil War.

136
00:09:15,721 --> 00:09:18,740
The soldiers became known as butternuts, a reference to the

137
00:09:18,741 --> 00:09:20,620
color and local walnut variety.

138
00:09:20,621 --> 00:09:27,620
After the hulls have been removed, the nuts are cleaned and inspected before being transported

139
00:09:27,621 --> 00:09:31,620
to Diamond's Stockton plant.

140
00:09:31,621 --> 00:09:36,620
Nearly half of all walnuts are immediately processed, prepped and shipped for the holiday season,

141
00:09:36,621 --> 00:09:39,620
when many nuts are traditionally eaten.

142
00:09:39,621 --> 00:09:44,620
The remaining tons are held in vast on-site storage facilities.

143
00:09:44,621 --> 00:09:51,100
Here we have 18 vessels that are refrigerated and they hold about 3 million pounds.

144
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:54,920
And then we have three other vessels that hold 20 million pounds.

145
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:58,480
So we can store about 110 million pounds of walnuts on site.

146
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:06,100
Walnuts can last for four months at room temperature, but nearly 24 months when kept in a controlled environment.

147
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:12,900
When needed, stored walnuts are drawn from the vessels and enter a labyrinth of conveyors and chutes.

148
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:22,280
80% of them enter Diamond's mechanized cracking system, which shatters upwards of 1.1 million

149
00:10:22,281 --> 00:10:26,660
pounds of walnuts a day.

150
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:31,180
Once the shell is sheared from the kernel, an advanced aeration system sucks the lighter

151
00:10:31,181 --> 00:10:37,020
density shell particles upward, while the kernels continue on below.

152
00:10:37,021 --> 00:10:42,100
The kernels then fall onto a conveyor belt, traveling at 500 feet per minute, and begin

153
00:10:42,101 --> 00:10:43,645
laser sorting process.

154
00:10:43,646 --> 00:10:48,140
As each kernel passes through the scanning zone, the laser makes

155
00:10:48,141 --> 00:10:56,660
two near instantaneous passes. The first scan alone is 99.8% effective.

156
00:10:56,661 --> 00:10:58,820
Based on the scan,

157
00:10:58,821 --> 00:11:03,300
the computer triggers small air sprayers to eject unwanted kernels and remaining shell

158
00:11:03,301 --> 00:11:04,300
bits.

159
00:11:04,301 --> 00:11:10,540
If you'd gone into our plant 20 years ago, you'd have seen literally 30 or 40 tables

160
00:11:10,541 --> 00:11:13,720
with people standing at them and the nuts being hand sorted

161
00:11:13,721 --> 00:11:15,440
to reach the quality.

162
00:11:15,441 --> 00:11:18,180
And what you find is that people can only sort defects

163
00:11:18,181 --> 00:11:20,520
to a certain level of cleanliness.

164
00:11:20,521 --> 00:11:22,580
By putting the laser sorters in,

165
00:11:22,581 --> 00:11:26,100
we were actually able to double the capacity

166
00:11:26,101 --> 00:11:30,980
of our showing plant and improve the quality.

167
00:11:30,981 --> 00:11:33,620
Kernels removed from the flow because of color

168
00:11:33,621 --> 00:11:36,180
are chopped and diced for baking goods, candies,

169
00:11:36,181 --> 00:11:39,220
and ice cream, or ground to create walnut oil for cooking.

170
00:11:40,540 --> 00:11:49,140
Once sorting is completed, most whole kernels are sent immediately to packaging, while a growing percentage are dedicated to Diamond's new nut snacks.

171
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:57,180
Roasted nuts, for example, are prepared using two different methods, dry roast and oil roast.

172
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:01,640
The dry roaster, that's basically like a big oven.

173
00:12:01,641 --> 00:12:08,080
With the oil wheelchair, we're using really a new technology where the nuts are put on a conveyor,

174
00:12:08,081 --> 00:12:16,460
and oil that's typically 280 to 300 degrees is cascaded over the nuts as they pass through the chamber.

175
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:22,340
And that hot oil, as it passes over the nuts, cooks the nuts in a very uniform way.

176
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:28,480
Today, walnuts and almonds are California's leading agricultural export.

177
00:12:28,481 --> 00:12:38,684
But the West Coast isn't the only place where nuts and technology come together to make a better product, even if that product happens to stick to the roof of your

178
00:12:38,685 --> 00:12:39,385
mouth.

179
00:12:41,760 --> 00:12:50,500
It's a staple in 75% of all American homes, and its rich flavor and creamy texture are widely popular in nations around the world.

180
00:12:51,140 --> 00:12:53,160
It is, of course, peanut butter.

181
00:12:53,161 --> 00:12:58,740
Peter Pan has been making peanut butter for nearly 90 years and is one of the founding

182
00:12:58,741 --> 00:13:01,280
brands of the industry.

183
00:13:01,281 --> 00:13:06,120
But while the company's roots date back decades, the Peter Pan factory in Sylvester, Georgia

184
00:13:06,121 --> 00:13:09,700
is anything but antique.

185
00:13:09,701 --> 00:13:14,040
Not only does the facility have one of the world's largest oven roasters, but it also

186
00:13:14,041 --> 00:13:17,400
features sophisticated grinding, cooling and packaging systems.

187
00:13:17,401 --> 00:13:19,937
It's an extremely automated facility.

188
00:13:19,938 --> 00:13:23,160
The whole front end of the process, which would

189
00:13:23,161 --> 00:13:29,400
come from receiving of the peanuts all the way through cleaning, roasting, blanching,

190
00:13:29,401 --> 00:13:34,157
and the entire formulation process, we use only about 12 people to run them.

191
00:13:34,158 --> 00:13:34,858
12 people

192
00:13:34,859 --> 00:13:41,120
will produce close to 50,000 pounds of peanut butter an hour.

193
00:13:41,121 --> 00:13:45,800
Remarkably, Peter Pan peanut butter has used the same basic ingredients since its inception

194
00:13:45,801 --> 00:13:46,542
in 1928.

195
00:13:46,543 --> 00:13:54,800
Sugar, salt, a blended oil-based stabilizer that ensures the natural oils don't separate,

196
00:13:54,801 --> 00:13:57,148
and 90% Georgia peanuts.

197
00:13:57,149 --> 00:14:03,800
Peanuts are native to South America, where they were among history's

198
00:14:03,801 --> 00:14:06,015
earliest domesticated foods.

199
00:14:06,016 --> 00:14:11,000
Centuries later, Portuguese traders carried peanuts from Brazil

200
00:14:11,001 --> 00:14:17,880
to Africa, where the crop remains a vital commodity even today.

201
00:14:17,881 --> 00:14:22,680
Sadly it was via the African slave trade that peanuts returned across the Atlantic to America's

202
00:14:22,681 --> 00:14:26,200
shores.

203
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:29,240
After the Civil War, peanuts became commonplace.

204
00:14:29,241 --> 00:14:34,240
But peanut butter didn't emerge until the late 1890s, and contrary to popular belief,

205
00:14:34,241 --> 00:14:38,880
it was not invented by George Washington Carver.

206
00:14:38,881 --> 00:14:44,200
Carver's pioneering peanut research, especially the process known as nitrogen fixation, made

207
00:14:44,201 --> 00:14:48,400
peanuts a truly viable crop for southern farmers.

208
00:14:48,401 --> 00:14:53,300
And Carver is rightly credited with devising hundreds of additional uses for peanuts, just

209
00:14:53,301 --> 00:14:55,560
not peanut butter.

210
00:14:55,561 --> 00:15:03,560
Instead, a Midwestern doctor debuted peanut butter at the 1893 Chicago Exposition, selling

211
00:15:03,561 --> 00:15:06,400
it as a healthy resource for patients.

212
00:15:06,401 --> 00:15:11,900
He needed a nutritious source of protein that they could also digest and handle and actually

213
00:15:11,901 --> 00:15:13,900
want to eat.

214
00:15:13,901 --> 00:15:17,785
In 1895, Dr. John H. Kellogg and his brother W.K.

215
00:15:17,786 --> 00:15:20,640
patented their own variety of peanut

216
00:15:20,641 --> 00:15:26,160
butter, another in their long list of food inventions, including Kellogg's now famous

217
00:15:26,161 --> 00:15:27,160
cereals.

218
00:15:27,161 --> 00:15:32,660
The big change between the peanut butter he was making and peanut butter as you know

219
00:15:32,661 --> 00:15:35,540
it today is the roasting process.

220
00:15:35,541 --> 00:15:40,880
He actually used a steam process that didn't generate that roasted flavor that you'd know today.

221
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:46,740
For the next 20 years, dozens of local producers competed in the peanut butter market.

222
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:52,600
But the shelf life for these products was short, and the natural oils in the butter separated.

223
00:15:53,320 --> 00:15:59,040
In the early 1920s, Peter Pan became the first company to market a stabilized peanut butter.

224
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:03,860
This new peanut butter could last on store shelves for more than a year.

225
00:16:03,861 --> 00:16:12,860
And with it, a national industry was born.

226
00:16:12,861 --> 00:16:18,460
Peanuts are planted after the last frost in April.

227
00:16:18,461 --> 00:16:24,600
The peanut plant is unusual because it flowers above ground, but fruits below ground.

228
00:16:24,601 --> 00:16:29,140
The flowers pollinate themselves, then the petals fall off as the peanut ovary begins

229
00:16:29,141 --> 00:16:30,140
to form.

230
00:16:30,141 --> 00:16:35,740
This budding ovary, called a peg, grows away from the plant on a vine that penetrates the soil.

231
00:16:37,020 --> 00:16:40,680
The peanuts mature below the ground and are harvested in the fall.

232
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:46,760
Tractors armed with digger shakers move steadily through the fields.

233
00:16:48,220 --> 00:16:51,240
Their long blades dig into the ground and cut the taproot,

234
00:16:51,840 --> 00:16:55,380
after which the plant is lifted and shaken until the peanuts fall free.

235
00:16:55,381 --> 00:17:01,540
Left to dry for two or three days, the peanuts are then collected, shelled, and loaded into

236
00:17:01,541 --> 00:17:06,800
trucks for distribution, including to Peter Pan peanut butter.

237
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:12,700
We will bring in well over 100 million pounds of peanuts each year, and during our peak

238
00:17:12,701 --> 00:17:16,280
season, we'll actually produce well over half a million jars a day.

239
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:23,220
Once the raw peanuts arrive, it's time to start the highly automated process.

240
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:30,320
They're cleaned and then roasted in an oven that measures more than 150 feet long.

241
00:17:31,520 --> 00:17:34,840
We'll roast approximately 40,000 pounds of peanuts per hour,

242
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:39,060
and we'll roast those to bring out a roasted flavor and also give color to the peanut butter.

243
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:43,900
After blanching and color sorting, the peanuts are ground twice,

244
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:48,660
then later sent through a homogenizer to ensure the smoothest possible product.

245
00:17:48,661 --> 00:17:56,340
The peanut butter is taken at very high pressure, over 5,000 pounds of pressure, through this

246
00:17:56,341 --> 00:17:58,880
homogenizer, and that whips it into a butter.

247
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:05,760
Each stage is monitored closely in the factory's central computerized hub, including the rapid

248
00:18:05,761 --> 00:18:10,200
cooling of the peanut butter to ensure its integrity and prepare it for the jar fillers.

249
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:15,220
During our peak season, we'll produce well over half a million jars of peanut butter

250
00:18:15,221 --> 00:18:15,921
per day.

251
00:18:15,922 --> 00:18:21,880
Each jar of standard-sized peanut butter will have a pound of peanuts per jar.

252
00:18:21,881 --> 00:18:25,760
And how do they make crunchy peanut butter?

253
00:18:25,761 --> 00:18:30,920
Crunchy peanut butter is almost the same as a creamy, except for one primary difference.

254
00:18:30,921 --> 00:18:34,560
When the peanuts are taken from the finished roasted nut bin that's already been cleaned

255
00:18:34,561 --> 00:18:39,660
and color sorted, some will go to the paste stream and some will go to be chopped into

256
00:18:39,661 --> 00:18:43,200
the size we want for our crunchy granules.

257
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:47,280
Those two different streams of peanuts will meet back just prior to the filler, where

258
00:18:47,281 --> 00:18:52,520
we'll blend those crunchy granules right back in with our creamy peanut butter.

259
00:18:52,521 --> 00:18:57,100
Since the 1890s, when it was first invented, peanut butter has been an important staple

260
00:18:57,101 --> 00:19:02,480
of the American diet.

261
00:19:02,481 --> 00:19:05,420
Yet its medicinal roots have not been forgotten.

262
00:19:05,421 --> 00:19:09,500
And today, a new peanut butter product saves lives.

263
00:19:09,501 --> 00:19:11,840
It's called Plumpy Nut.

264
00:19:11,841 --> 00:19:17,220
For decades, international relief organizations like UNICEF have sought to stave off malnutrition

265
00:19:17,221 --> 00:19:21,080
and starvation with powdered milk formulas.

266
00:19:21,081 --> 00:19:24,360
But on the ground, there were many challenges.

267
00:19:24,361 --> 00:19:28,040
Feeding children with therapeutic milk had a couple of disadvantages.

268
00:19:28,041 --> 00:19:31,200
One, milk you have to mix with water.

269
00:19:31,201 --> 00:19:34,800
Water is one of the hardest things in emergencies because if you don't have clean water, you

270
00:19:34,801 --> 00:19:38,120
actually aggravate malnutrition, you aggravate diarrhea.

271
00:19:38,121 --> 00:19:43,000
So they wanted a product that would be packaged, that was safe, to which you didn't have to add water.

272
00:19:43,080 --> 00:19:44,320
And that's how they came up with this.

273
00:19:45,860 --> 00:19:50,680
Introduced in 1999, plumpy nut comes in 500 kilocalorie packets.

274
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:55,260
It can be eaten right from the back and has a shelf life of two years.

275
00:19:56,740 --> 00:20:02,660
Anyone who has a child knows it's really, really hard to get a child to eat when that child does not want to eat.

276
00:20:02,661 --> 00:20:04,620
And malnourished kids do not want to eat.

277
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:07,880
What's amazing about plumpy nut is that you hand that to a child.

278
00:20:07,881 --> 00:20:10,820
you put a little bit in its mouth, and the child starts eating.

279
00:20:11,120 --> 00:20:13,060
So it makes our job so much easier.

280
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:16,840
Because of the high protein content of peanuts,

281
00:20:17,280 --> 00:20:21,180
Plumpy Nut can reverse malnutrition in children in just over four weeks.

282
00:20:22,480 --> 00:20:25,960
Normally, recovery from malnutrition takes a very long time,

283
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:29,380
and what we've seen historically is that kids start to do a bit better,

284
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:30,460
and then they fall back.

285
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:34,920
What we've seen with Plumpy Nut is that about 75% of the kids just get better.

286
00:20:34,921 --> 00:20:39,000
It takes about 40 days. It's about $50 per treatment.

287
00:20:39,340 --> 00:20:41,980
With a success rate, it's a great product.

288
00:20:42,440 --> 00:20:47,240
So there are huge advantages for the mother, for the family, and for us, UNICEF,

289
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:49,680
making sure that we're reaching as many kids as we can.

290
00:20:51,280 --> 00:20:54,920
Imagine all that hope from one little nut.

291
00:20:58,380 --> 00:21:02,400
Crack them, hammer them, crush them with a rock.

292
00:21:02,401 --> 00:21:10,000
All that stands between you and your favorite nut are the shells, and as the remarkable

293
00:21:10,001 --> 00:21:14,940
collection of 5,000 historic nutcrackers at the Leavenworth Nut Museum shows.

294
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:18,360
We've come a long way from cracking nuts with simple stones.

295
00:21:19,480 --> 00:21:25,080
But for all their style and design, very few handcrackers in the museum, or in the world,

296
00:21:25,260 --> 00:21:30,220
can handle the hardest shell in the nut kingdom, the macadamia.

297
00:21:30,221 --> 00:21:36,600
It takes about 300 psi, pounds per square inch of pressure to actually crack those,

298
00:21:36,680 --> 00:21:38,420
so they're a very, very hard nut to crack.

299
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:44,680
No company in the world is more familiar with just how hard the macadamia shell is

300
00:21:44,681 --> 00:21:47,140
than Hawaii's Mauna Loa,

301
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:51,280
which has been growing and processing the nuts for nearly 60 years.

302
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:58,000
Comprised of more than 10,000 acres throughout the islands,

303
00:21:58,001 --> 00:22:03,040
Mauna Loa receives 33 million pounds of in-shell macadamia nuts annually.

304
00:22:04,660 --> 00:22:10,620
And of those, 90% will be cracked using a proprietary high-tech cracking system

305
00:22:10,621 --> 00:22:13,020
designed specifically for macadamias.

306
00:22:13,960 --> 00:22:20,820
We crack around 8,500 to 9,000 pounds per hour without seven crackers.

307
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:25,140
The challenge? To not simply shatter the shell,

308
00:22:25,141 --> 00:22:28,420
but keep a high percentage of the whole nut kernel intact.

309
00:22:31,460 --> 00:22:35,040
In the old days, the only choice was to crack the nuts with hammers,

310
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:38,600
one nut at a time, as people had done for centuries.

311
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:46,540
Although closely associated with Hawaii,

312
00:22:47,220 --> 00:22:49,620
macadamias are native to the Australian rainforest,

313
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:54,480
where the hard shell protected the nut from a host of tropical predators and diseases.

314
00:22:55,140 --> 00:23:02,820
The macadamia's rich flavor was prized by Aboriginal tribes, who simply smashed open

315
00:23:02,821 --> 00:23:07,480
the nuts with rocks featuring small divots.

316
00:23:07,481 --> 00:23:14,960
In 1858, a colonial botanist, Ferdinand von Mueller, was the first to catalog the species,

317
00:23:14,961 --> 00:23:19,380
naming the nut in honor of fellow explorer John Macadam, who had recently perished at

318
00:23:19,381 --> 00:23:22,720
sea.

319
00:23:22,721 --> 00:23:26,620
The first macadamias didn't arrive in Hawaii until nearly three decades later,

320
00:23:27,340 --> 00:23:32,400
when entrepreneur George Purvis decided they would make an attractive addition to the Hawaiian landscape.

321
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:39,360
Purvis is actually credited with bringing the first plants back to us from Australia back to Hawaii.

322
00:23:39,880 --> 00:23:42,600
The interest at that time was really in its ornamental value.

323
00:23:42,780 --> 00:23:44,520
It's a very beautiful looking tree.

324
00:23:44,900 --> 00:23:48,580
But when he discovered that the nuts from them could be quite tasty,

325
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:52,040
that began interest in commercialization of the nuts themselves.

326
00:23:52,720 --> 00:23:59,480
Hawaii's climate and rich volcanic soil created an ideal environment.

327
00:23:59,481 --> 00:24:04,600
But in the decades to follow, both the tree and the nut proved surprisingly difficult for

328
00:24:04,601 --> 00:24:06,100
commercial cultivation.

329
00:24:06,101 --> 00:24:10,760
There's an outer fibrous husk and then there's this hard shell.

330
00:24:10,761 --> 00:24:17,180
So the nut itself, when it falls from the tree, actually only about 15% of it is edible.

331
00:24:17,181 --> 00:24:21,600
And so it's a very expensive nut to grow, a very expensive nut to process, and you get

332
00:24:21,601 --> 00:24:24,480
very little kernel at the end of the day.

333
00:24:24,481 --> 00:24:28,160
Only when modern cracking technology emerged in the 1980s

334
00:24:28,161 --> 00:24:33,220
did the macadamia industry begin to erupt.

335
00:24:33,221 --> 00:24:36,420
The cracking process begins in a series of drying tanks

336
00:24:36,421 --> 00:24:39,560
that reduce the nut's natural moisture from 20%

337
00:24:39,561 --> 00:24:42,920
to less than 2%.

338
00:24:42,921 --> 00:24:45,960
This shrinks the kernel within and renders the hard shell

339
00:24:45,961 --> 00:24:47,500
more brittle.

340
00:24:47,501 --> 00:24:50,920
We have about 48 tanks, and we hold about 4 and 1

341
00:24:50,921 --> 00:24:54,300
half million pounds of drying shell.

342
00:24:54,301 --> 00:24:56,760
When it come over here, we start it off

343
00:24:56,761 --> 00:24:59,840
at a very low temperature, about 100 degrees.

344
00:24:59,841 --> 00:25:02,480
As soon as the moisture starts to go down,

345
00:25:02,481 --> 00:25:06,400
we'll ramp it up to about 125 for about another two days,

346
00:25:06,401 --> 00:25:10,100
and we'll finish it off at about 140 to 145

347
00:25:10,101 --> 00:25:15,200
for a total drying cycle of about 10 days.

348
00:25:15,201 --> 00:25:17,320
Once dried, the now brittle macadamias

349
00:25:17,321 --> 00:25:20,080
enter one of Mauna Loa's seven separate crackers.

350
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:25,240
Inside each cracker, two metal rollers

351
00:25:25,241 --> 00:25:27,100
spin at a constant high speed.

352
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:31,020
The individual macadamia nut falls into a set gap

353
00:25:31,021 --> 00:25:33,180
between the rollers, cracking the shell

354
00:25:33,181 --> 00:25:34,500
along its vertical seam.

355
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:40,280
The result, more than seven million pounds

356
00:25:40,281 --> 00:25:42,580
of shelled whole macadamia kernels.

357
00:25:44,280 --> 00:25:46,560
These kernels are then either sent to packaging,

358
00:25:47,320 --> 00:25:51,000
or further processed into one of Mauna Loa's many product lines.

359
00:25:57,520 --> 00:26:01,520
And in case you're wondering what becomes of all the broken shells...

360
00:26:01,521 --> 00:26:05,700
We're very, very environmentally friendly processing.

361
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:09,500
The shell, after it's been cracked, is returned to our boiler,

362
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:13,500
where we burn it to produce steam and convert steam into energy.

363
00:26:13,501 --> 00:26:16,160
So we consume everything that comes out of the field.

364
00:26:16,161 --> 00:26:18,860
It's used either as a mainstream product or as a byproduct.

365
00:26:19,560 --> 00:26:21,540
And Mauna Loa isn't alone.

366
00:26:22,280 --> 00:26:26,780
The truth is, science now employs cracked nutshells in many remarkable ways.

367
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:30,300
Three, two, one.

368
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:34,580
Booster ignition and liftoff of space shuttle Endeavour.

369
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:36,560
Eight minutes into every space shuttle mission,

370
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:40,220
the two solid fuel rocket boosters fall away.

371
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:43,520
Booster officer confirms good separation of the solid rocket boosters.

372
00:26:43,521 --> 00:26:50,640
Once back on Earth, the scorched and damaged boosters are recovered and then cleaned and

373
00:26:50,641 --> 00:26:53,039
prepped for the next mission.

374
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:57,920
To do so, NASA uses ground walnut shells, which are sprayed

375
00:26:57,921 --> 00:27:01,920
across the booster surfaces like sandblasting.

376
00:27:01,921 --> 00:27:05,920
Their product is supplied by ShellPro in Lodi,

377
00:27:05,921 --> 00:27:11,520
California, where more than 5 million pounds of almond, walnut, and apricot pit shells

378
00:27:11,521 --> 00:27:14,860
are ground every month.

379
00:27:14,861 --> 00:27:19,820
Walnut shell can be ground down to sizes that are very similar to sand.

380
00:27:19,821 --> 00:27:23,160
And as a result, you can use it in sandblasting equipment.

381
00:27:23,161 --> 00:27:24,200
You just substitute it.

382
00:27:24,201 --> 00:27:28,040
Because it's dense, it has that hardness they want,

383
00:27:28,041 --> 00:27:33,200
but it is not so hard and abrasive like silica sand would be

384
00:27:33,201 --> 00:27:37,240
that it's going to damage what they blast with it.

385
00:27:37,241 --> 00:27:40,140
But blast cleaning isn't the only application that ShellPro

386
00:27:40,141 --> 00:27:43,580
or its customers have discovered for the product.

387
00:27:43,581 --> 00:27:45,280
Any time you use one of those scrubs

388
00:27:45,281 --> 00:27:48,280
that has a gritty texture to it, you've probably

389
00:27:48,281 --> 00:27:53,640
used a soap that has walnut shell in it.

390
00:27:53,641 --> 00:27:56,820
Shell Pro has three different kinds of grinders, which

391
00:27:56,821 --> 00:28:00,180
produce 15 standard grades of ground shell,

392
00:28:00,181 --> 00:28:03,660
and can be modified to create custom grades when needed.

393
00:28:03,661 --> 00:28:07,320
This line behind me right here is producing material

394
00:28:07,321 --> 00:28:11,000
that will be going into the ground while they're drilling for oil.

395
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:13,740
When the material comes in, it looks like this.

396
00:28:13,800 --> 00:28:15,280
It's just broken walnut shell.

397
00:28:16,060 --> 00:28:19,540
And when we put it in the bag, it looks something like this.

398
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:24,840
Today, ground walnut shells are used as a packing material for dynamite,

399
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:27,880
for water filtration, to make plywood,

400
00:28:28,600 --> 00:28:30,460
and even Hollywood special effects.

401
00:28:31,080 --> 00:28:34,800
We had one company that shipped large quantities of it

402
00:28:34,801 --> 00:28:38,540
over actually to the Middle East to be able to reproduce sandstorms.

403
00:28:38,541 --> 00:28:42,140
Even though they got a lot of sand over there, they wanted a safer environment.

404
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:46,160
Innovation has become a hallmark of the nut industry.

405
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:54,380
But for scientists and breeders, even America's oldest nut, the pecan, still has room for improvement.

406
00:28:57,920 --> 00:29:02,880
The Seychelles Islands, 150 miles off the coast of Africa,

407
00:29:02,881 --> 00:29:06,020
a place as pristine as it is remote,

408
00:29:06,540 --> 00:29:10,380
where the ecological balance has been maintained for millions of years.

409
00:29:11,640 --> 00:29:15,600
Here, atop trees that tower more than 150 feet in the air,

410
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:19,320
grows the world's largest nut, the coco de mer,

411
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:21,580
which takes seven years to develop

412
00:29:21,581 --> 00:29:26,100
and will weigh more than 70 pounds when it finally comes crashing to the ground.

413
00:29:27,140 --> 00:29:29,080
Too heavy to float to other shores,

414
00:29:29,620 --> 00:29:32,000
the coco de mer has never escaped its island home.

415
00:29:32,740 --> 00:29:36,600
Marooned and isolated, this remarkable nut grows nowhere else in the world.

416
00:29:37,220 --> 00:29:38,820
It is a variety of one.

417
00:29:42,460 --> 00:29:47,460
About 10,000 miles away in the Brazos River Valley at College Station, Texas,

418
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:54,940
the USDA Pecan Breeding Program houses the single largest repository of pecan varieties in the world.

419
00:29:56,160 --> 00:29:59,760
Unlike the Coco de Mer, the pecan has a rich history,

420
00:29:59,761 --> 00:30:03,100
featuring many varieties filled with genetic diversity.

421
00:30:05,640 --> 00:30:08,620
Pecans are the most famous nuts indigenous to North America.

422
00:30:09,920 --> 00:30:14,180
Originating in the South, native trees spread from Mexico to Illinois,

423
00:30:14,181 --> 00:30:16,420
where pioneers first encountered them.

424
00:30:18,660 --> 00:30:22,580
Analyzing how those native pecans adapted to different climates and locales

425
00:30:22,581 --> 00:30:27,400
and their genetic story offers both scientific and commercial opportunities

426
00:30:27,401 --> 00:30:28,920
for today's pecan industry.

427
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:37,560
If we understand the mechanisms that the trees have adapted over time to survive in their areas,

428
00:30:37,660 --> 00:30:41,960
we can do a better job of choosing those adaptations and recombining them.

429
00:30:44,160 --> 00:30:47,540
Pecans are now an $800 million a year industry,

430
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:52,840
and processors employ the latest crackers, color sorters, and packaging equipment.

431
00:30:52,841 --> 00:31:00,840
Yet pecan growers can no longer rely on the native orchards that once fueled the industry.

432
00:31:00,841 --> 00:31:06,840
Instead, growers have begun to ask, can we make a better nut?

433
00:31:06,841 --> 00:31:11,840
Pecans only bear every other year, so we have been struggling with this alternate bearing

434
00:31:11,841 --> 00:31:14,176
since the very beginning of time.

435
00:31:14,177 --> 00:31:19,840
But we do now have growers that can go in and produce quality pecans every year.

436
00:31:19,841 --> 00:31:22,160
And that's a very difficult task.

437
00:31:23,820 --> 00:31:29,760
Thirty years ago, common knowledge held that a new pecan tree didn't bear a commercial crop for ten years.

438
00:31:30,840 --> 00:31:36,480
Today, that time has been cut in half, in large part because of genetically improved cultivars

439
00:31:36,481 --> 00:31:39,200
and a breeding process called crossing.

440
00:31:40,940 --> 00:31:44,380
We may have one parent, for instance, that's very high-yielding,

441
00:31:44,381 --> 00:31:47,140
but it's susceptible to scab disease.

442
00:31:47,141 --> 00:31:54,640
So we might take that one parent that's very high yielding and cross it to another parent that doesn't yield so well,

443
00:31:54,740 --> 00:31:56,740
but has extremely good scab resistance.

444
00:31:57,220 --> 00:32:01,920
And then we grow out those progeny, and then we start testing them for both of those characteristics.

445
00:32:02,480 --> 00:32:03,734
In the 1880s, E.E.

446
00:32:03,735 --> 00:32:09,240
Reesian, an Englishman living in Sansaba, Texas, was the first to use crossing,

447
00:32:09,720 --> 00:32:12,900
and in so doing, launched the commercial pecan industry.

448
00:32:13,880 --> 00:32:20,020
He'd have a contest every fall where he would pay $5 for the best barrel of quality pecans.

449
00:32:20,340 --> 00:32:24,200
And one day he found a sensational barrel of pecans.

450
00:32:24,320 --> 00:32:26,360
He asked the man to take him to the tree.

451
00:32:26,361 --> 00:32:32,440
He took that tree, collected 1,000 nuts, planted the nuts, and released over 30 cultivars,

452
00:32:32,560 --> 00:32:36,720
some of which we grow today, the most important of which was the western pecan,

453
00:32:36,840 --> 00:32:39,040
which is the number one pecan planted in the world.

454
00:32:39,041 --> 00:32:45,160
To create a new cultivar, breeders collect the male pollen of the first desired variety.

455
00:32:46,900 --> 00:32:50,260
Then they bag the female stigma of the other desired variety,

456
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:54,060
preventing any unwanted male pollen from reaching it.

457
00:32:55,180 --> 00:33:01,440
Then we wait about a week or 10 days until the stigmatic surface of these flowers become receptive.

458
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:06,620
And at that time, we take pollen and we put it in our pollination bag like this,

459
00:33:06,621 --> 00:33:08,640
and then put this together.

460
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:13,060
Then we shake out a little bit of the pollen in our glass loop there,

461
00:33:13,800 --> 00:33:15,360
not very much, just a small amount.

462
00:33:15,460 --> 00:33:20,960
We insert that needle up through the base of the bag and give it a squeeze.

463
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:24,320
Then we get a cloud of pollen in that bag,

464
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:29,340
and it's just about the right amount to effectuate pollination and get nuts set.

465
00:33:30,500 --> 00:33:34,000
In the fall, the potential cultivar will yield its first nuts,

466
00:33:34,001 --> 00:33:38,400
which the breeder then plants to see if the future tree possesses the desired

467
00:33:38,401 --> 00:33:46,600
traits. That can take years. It's about a 20-year process from the time we make

468
00:33:46,601 --> 00:33:52,720
a cross until the time we release a new USDA variety. Since the breeding program

469
00:33:52,721 --> 00:33:59,400
was founded in 1930, USDA breeders have released 25 new varieties, three in the

470
00:33:59,401 --> 00:34:00,540
last 10 years.

471
00:34:00,541 --> 00:34:06,400
Once approved, new cultivars are made available to tree growers like Hal

472
00:34:06,401 --> 00:34:10,352
Birdall, owner of Birdall's Pecan Farm and Nursery.

473
00:34:10,354 --> 00:34:13,220
For Birdall, the goal isn't to create

474
00:34:13,221 --> 00:34:18,480
new cultivars, but to adapt and propagate existing varieties to the day-to-day practicalities

475
00:34:18,481 --> 00:34:19,580
of commercial growing.

476
00:34:19,581 --> 00:34:24,883
In the nursery here, there's around 75,000 or 80,000 trees.

477
00:34:24,884 --> 00:34:27,580
In the orchard, there's around

478
00:34:27,581 --> 00:34:31,080
six or seven thousand trees, something like that.

479
00:34:31,081 --> 00:34:34,220
Often this requires grafting and forcing two distinct

480
00:34:34,221 --> 00:34:36,680
varieties together to take advantage of their unique

481
00:34:36,681 --> 00:34:38,020
characteristics.

482
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:45,140
Grafting is an art that dates back centuries.

483
00:34:45,141 --> 00:34:49,840
In 1846, a slave named Antoine was the first to graft young

484
00:34:49,841 --> 00:34:53,780
pecan trees at the famous Oak Galley Plantation outside of

485
00:34:53,781 --> 00:34:56,740
New Orleans.

486
00:34:56,741 --> 00:35:00,040
This particular tree here was budded last summer in August.

487
00:35:00,320 --> 00:35:02,360
We stripped the bark off the native tree there,

488
00:35:02,660 --> 00:35:05,800
and that causes this little bud to come out and start growing.

489
00:35:06,180 --> 00:35:10,520
That forced all the sap that was coming up to come out of this bud patch,

490
00:35:10,960 --> 00:35:13,800
and you can see now it has came out and started growing,

491
00:35:14,340 --> 00:35:18,140
and by the end of this growing season, this tree will be probably 6 feet tall.

492
00:35:19,040 --> 00:35:21,800
Today, those grafting techniques are supplemented with fertilizers,

493
00:35:22,760 --> 00:35:25,980
advanced irrigation, and in-ground growing containers,

494
00:35:25,981 --> 00:35:28,300
rather than pots above the ground.

495
00:35:29,700 --> 00:35:31,620
And the results have been remarkable.

496
00:35:32,360 --> 00:35:34,960
What we're looking at here is a tree a year later

497
00:35:34,961 --> 00:35:36,320
from what we were just looking at.

498
00:35:36,780 --> 00:35:40,700
This is where that graft or bud was put on last spring.

499
00:35:41,100 --> 00:35:46,260
It grew all the way up to here about 6 feet tall last summer.

500
00:35:46,760 --> 00:35:48,640
And now you see it's come out this spring.

501
00:35:49,120 --> 00:35:51,380
And this is the amount of growth it's grown in one month

502
00:35:51,381 --> 00:35:53,700
from right here where my finger is to there,

503
00:35:53,701 --> 00:35:55,960
which is 2 1⁄2 or 3 feet.

504
00:35:57,080 --> 00:35:59,240
Today, the pecan industry is thriving,

505
00:35:59,920 --> 00:36:02,480
in Texas and throughout the South and Southwest.

506
00:36:03,920 --> 00:36:07,600
But from a genetics point of view, it's just getting started.

507
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:10,320
We've only been growing pecans 150 years.

508
00:36:10,400 --> 00:36:13,880
We don't have, like, olives or grapes or apples.

509
00:36:14,020 --> 00:36:17,180
We don't have 3,000 or 4,000 years of background information.

510
00:36:17,360 --> 00:36:18,740
We're at the very beginning.

511
00:36:18,741 --> 00:36:28,400
But pecans aren't the only industry undergoing a modern facelift, as technology makes these ancient favorites a rising star.

512
00:36:33,740 --> 00:36:36,500
Entrepreneurship has always been a hallmark of the nut industry.

513
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:45,200
Nowhere in recent years is that spirit more evident than in the pistachio business, including Paramount Farms,

514
00:36:45,201 --> 00:36:49,800
now the largest integrated grower, processor, and distributor in the world.

515
00:36:53,600 --> 00:36:57,100
Pistachios are the youngest commercial nut industry in the United States,

516
00:36:57,580 --> 00:36:59,740
beginning only in the 1960s.

517
00:37:01,680 --> 00:37:05,400
For centuries, Iran was the world's foremost producer.

518
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:10,520
But the revolution in 1979 led to new opportunities for American growers.

519
00:37:10,521 --> 00:37:18,120
Today, Paramount Farms has 28,000 acres of pistachio orchards throughout the San Joaquin

520
00:37:18,121 --> 00:37:22,760
Valley, the largest in the Western Hemisphere.

521
00:37:22,761 --> 00:37:28,840
Like all nuts, pistachios pose unique challenges for growers and processors.

522
00:37:28,841 --> 00:37:33,980
Beginning in early May, pistachios grow in a grape-like cluster.

523
00:37:33,981 --> 00:37:38,840
During the next three months, a hull, shell, and kernel form within each nutlet.

524
00:37:38,841 --> 00:37:45,840
Once the shell is hardened, the kernel itself will begin to fill in July.

525
00:37:45,841 --> 00:37:48,500
That filling process takes about six weeks.

526
00:37:48,501 --> 00:37:53,920
It actually puts pressure on the shell to get it to split.

527
00:37:53,921 --> 00:37:59,680
Once split open, shakers bring them cascading down onto large tarps that ensure the pistachios

528
00:37:59,681 --> 00:38:05,760
never touch the ground.

529
00:38:05,761 --> 00:38:10,900
Unlike walnuts or almonds, a pistachio's hull is still wet when ripe.

530
00:38:10,901 --> 00:38:16,280
Left on the ground, that wet hull will begin to dry, causing discoloration.

531
00:38:16,281 --> 00:38:19,880
When I ran, typically, the pistachios would hit the ground and they would sun dry them.

532
00:38:19,881 --> 00:38:25,580
Well, when you sun dry pistachios, that hull would adhere to the shell, and it also stains

533
00:38:25,581 --> 00:38:26,580
the shell.

534
00:38:26,581 --> 00:38:31,400
So what they did is they dyed them all red, so you couldn't see it and they looked consistent.

535
00:38:31,401 --> 00:38:33,000
So we don't dye pistachios red.

536
00:38:35,760 --> 00:38:41,260
Fresh from the trees, the pistachio hulls are removed with a powerful water flush.

537
00:38:41,261 --> 00:38:46,160
We do that with about 10,000 gallons of water a minute. So in the course of eight

538
00:38:46,161 --> 00:38:48,700
weeks we'll use about three to four hundred million gallons of water in our

539
00:38:48,701 --> 00:38:51,680
hulling operations at all our facilities. The product then goes from the

540
00:38:51,681 --> 00:38:55,600
hulling operation through a sink float process. It's the first real quality split.

541
00:38:55,601 --> 00:38:58,820
What we're looking at there is to separate the mature from the less mature.

542
00:38:58,821 --> 00:39:03,420
Mature stuff sinks and the immature stuff floats. Real, real crazy idea but it works

543
00:39:03,421 --> 00:39:04,220
very well.

544
00:39:04,221 --> 00:39:10,380
After being dried, the pistachios enter a pin sorter in order to separate the

545
00:39:10,381 --> 00:39:14,239
open-shelled nuts from the closed ones.

546
00:39:14,240 --> 00:39:19,880
Thousands of tiny pins rotating in a large drum hook into

547
00:39:19,881 --> 00:39:25,140
the shell openings and lift them onto an upper conveyor while the closed shells tumble away.

548
00:39:25,141 --> 00:39:37,560
The pistachios are electronically color-sorted, hand-sorted, and then sized before roasting.

549
00:39:39,480 --> 00:39:45,000
Each year, Paramount harvests 20 million wet pounds of pistachios per day from the field,

550
00:39:45,520 --> 00:39:49,140
while processing 3 to 4 million pounds per week.

551
00:39:49,141 --> 00:39:56,140
Today, pistachios are the second-fastest developing nut industry in the country.

552
00:39:56,141 --> 00:40:01,140
99% of the pistachios grown in the United States are grown right here in the state of California.

553
00:40:01,141 --> 00:40:08,140
It's a crop that, in the last four years, in terms of dollar sales, has grown 28% compound annual growth.

554
00:40:08,141 --> 00:40:14,140
But none of it would have been possible without USDA breeder William Whitehouse,

555
00:40:14,141 --> 00:40:19,300
White House, who in 1930 was the first to bring pistachio nuts to the United States.

556
00:40:22,040 --> 00:40:27,180
In fact, so enamored of the pistachio was White House that he entered Iran through Russia

557
00:40:27,181 --> 00:40:30,180
at a time when Westerners were discouraged from traveling there.

558
00:40:32,240 --> 00:40:37,900
In six months, he collected 20 pounds of pistachios, which he smuggled back and planted.

559
00:40:38,460 --> 00:40:43,700
She brought these nuts back to Chico, California, which is about 600 miles north of where we're

560
00:40:43,701 --> 00:40:48,043
standing today. And then he worked with these nuts over 20 years.

561
00:40:48,044 --> 00:40:49,980
And after this whole process,

562
00:40:49,981 --> 00:40:52,856
he came up with three varieties that he liked.

563
00:40:52,857 --> 00:40:55,920
And then he spent another five years taking apart

564
00:40:55,921 --> 00:41:00,340
those varieties. And at the end, he came up with one variety, the Kerman variety.

565
00:41:02,060 --> 00:41:06,680
White House's Kerman became the bedrock of today's commercial pistachio industry.

566
00:41:08,220 --> 00:41:13,160
Kerman is still being planted in 99% of the orchards, and that is the white shell,

567
00:41:13,161 --> 00:41:17,660
Nice, big, open split, a green kernel with a nice, delicate taste.

568
00:41:17,980 --> 00:41:21,280
So what he did is truly amazing for California agriculture.

569
00:41:23,040 --> 00:41:27,780
That same entrepreneurial spirit still exists in many of today's nut producers,

570
00:41:28,540 --> 00:41:35,680
including those searching for sustainable alternative energy, such as biodiesel.

571
00:41:35,681 --> 00:41:44,520
The Indian Jatropha nut is nearly 50% oil and can be combusted as lamp fuel without

572
00:41:44,521 --> 00:41:47,160
being refined.

573
00:41:47,161 --> 00:41:55,520
It burns with a clear smoke-free flame that Indian farmers have used since the 17th century.

574
00:41:55,521 --> 00:42:00,480
Now American entrepreneurs have imported the nut to the United States, where the oil can

575
00:42:00,481 --> 00:42:03,600
be refined for use in modern diesel engines.

576
00:42:03,601 --> 00:42:10,740
It's not grown on prime agricultural land, it's grown on sandy, degraded wasteland.

577
00:42:10,741 --> 00:42:18,660
So we were able to get a USDA permit for bringing the seeds back to California last fall and

578
00:42:18,661 --> 00:42:24,200
start on the coast of California where the climatic conditions are such that we should

579
00:42:24,201 --> 00:42:28,480
be able to adapt a variety to this region fairly quickly.

580
00:42:28,481 --> 00:42:32,480
Currently, work with the Jatropha remains strictly research.

581
00:42:32,481 --> 00:42:36,480
But the plans are big and the hopes large.

582
00:42:36,481 --> 00:42:40,480
The amount of oil that a Jatropha plantation would produce

583
00:42:40,481 --> 00:42:45,480
after the first 18 months in a six-mile radius around a plant

584
00:42:45,481 --> 00:42:47,480
would generate about 3 million gallons a year.

585
00:42:47,481 --> 00:42:50,480
When the plant reaches maturity at 7 to 10 years,

586
00:42:50,481 --> 00:42:53,480
that's more like 30 million gallons a year.

587
00:42:53,481 --> 00:43:00,480
year. So a very large amount of oil out of a relatively small land.

588
00:43:00,481 --> 00:43:07,480
Practical, flavorful, nutritious, scientists now believe there are 30 million nut varieties.

589
00:43:07,481 --> 00:43:13,433
And who knows? One day, one of them might just change the world.

590
00:43:13,434 --> 00:43:15,480
How's that for a nutty

591
00:43:15,481 --> 00:43:16,480
idea?

